{
  "$schema": "../../../src/data/docs-authoring-schema.json",
  "id": "cameras",
  "title": "Camera projection, motion and controllers",
  "group": "3D and cameras",
  "summary": "Choose a projection, frame a composition and animate or interactively control the camera.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "set-an-intentional-camera",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "level": 2,
          "text": "Set an intentional camera"
        },
        {
          "type": "tabs",
          "tabs": [
            {
              "label": "Camera position",
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "example",
                  "example": "labs/pg-camera-move"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "Camera look-at",
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "example",
                  "example": "labs/pg-camera-look"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "Camera orbit",
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "example",
                  "example": "labs/pg-camera-orbit"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "Dolly zoom",
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "example",
                  "example": "labs/pg-camera-dolly"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Camera extends Entity and uses transform position/rotation. fov is used as radians by projection calculations; the constructor’s literal default is 45, so set a sensible radian FOV explicitly for a new perspective composition. lookAt accepts a point or Entity. Orthographic projection uses orthoScale and aspect ratio."
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "example": "labs/pg-camerascene"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Exact contracts: [Camera](/api-camera#camera), [CameraModel](/api-camera#camera), [FrameOptions](/api-camera#camera), [CameraMove](/api-animation-camera#cameramove), [CameraLookAt](/api-animation-camera#cameralookat), [CameraOrbit](/api-animation-camera#cameraorbit), [CameraDollyZoom](/api-animation-camera#cameradollyzoom)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "interactive-camera",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "level": 2,
          "text": "Compare camera projections"
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "example": "labs/lab-camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "projection-capabilities",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "level": 2,
          "text": "Projection capabilities"
        },
        {
          "type": "tabs",
          "tabs": [
            {
              "label": "Perspective",
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "example",
                  "example": "labs/pg-projection-perspective"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "Orthographic",
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "example",
                  "example": "labs/pg-projection-orthographic"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "Two Point",
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "example",
                  "example": "labs/pg-projection-two_point"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "PERSPECTIVE, ORTHOGRAPHIC and TWO_POINT have raster projection paths. FISHEYE and VR360 are enum values but getProjectionMatrix falls back to perspective for them; ray-generation behavior belongs to the ray-trace shaders and must be tested independently. The enum alone is not a promise of five complete raster projections."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "toOrtho and toPerspective preserve subject framing across projection changes at a selected distance. Lens shift adjusts the projection center. Near/far planes and reversed depth precision interact with the renderer’s depth conventions; do not replace the projection matrix with a conventional depth matrix without changing the matching pipeline."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "controller-lifecycles-and-units",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "level": 2,
          "text": "Controller lifecycles and units"
        },
        {
          "type": "tabs",
          "tabs": [
            {
              "label": "Follow",
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "example",
                  "example": "labs/pg-camera-follow"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "Deadzone",
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "example",
                  "example": "labs/pg-camera-deadzone"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "Damping",
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "example",
                  "example": "labs/pg-camera-damping"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "Zoom at point",
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "example",
                  "example": "labs/pg-camera-zoom-at"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "CameraController, OrbitCameraController and Camera2DController provide different interaction models. Register a single controller for the intended input mode and dispose its listeners when the host changes. Controller methods such as Camera2DController.moveTo/zoomTo/zoomToFit/shake use seconds, while its Animator integration uses AnimOptions milliseconds."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Camera2DController adds bounded panning, inertia, cursor-anchored zoom, touch gestures, deadzones, damping and target following. Bounds can account for the full viewport. SyncFromCamera restores the controller’s internal targets after an external camera change."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Exact contracts: [CameraController](/api-camera#cameracontroller), [OrbitCameraController](/api-camera#orbitcameracontroller), [Camera2DController](/api-camera#camera2dcontroller), [Camera2DAnimator](/api-camera#camera2dcontroller)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "depth-of-field",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "level": 2,
          "text": "Depth of field"
        },
        {
          "type": "tabs",
          "tabs": [
            {
              "label": "Path Tracing",
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "example",
                  "example": "labs/pg-path-tracing"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "Focus distance",
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "example",
                  "example": "labs/pg-depth-of-field"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "aperture and focusDist are used by ray-trace depth-of-field calculations. They are distinct from a post-process blur. Frame can update focusDist to the subject’s center. Larger blur effects and a thin focal plane need enough samples and suitable denoising to avoid confusing sampling noise with intended focus."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "continue-learning",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "level": 2,
          "text": "Continue learning"
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            "Continue with [3D geometry and materials](/three-d)."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Follow the guides in order using Previous and Next, or browse [Tutorials](/tutorials) for a practical lesson."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "prerequisites": [
    "[Transforms, anchors and pivots](/transforms)"
  ]
}
